    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is actually to *not* disable the interface. However, since Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" enables the interface and Juniper interfaces are enabled by default, the proper translation would be to simply omit any disable command. \\n\\nIf you specifically need the negation of shutdown (equivalent to \"no shutdown\"), there would typically be no command needed in Juniper (as interfaces are up by default), or you would use `delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable` if the interface was previously disabled. But the closest positive-action equivalent is shown above (though it\\'s actually the inverse of what you requested). \\n\\nThe most accurate translation for \"no shutdown\" in this context would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nThis removes any shutdown (disable) configuration from the interface.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is actually to *not* disable the interface. However, since Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" enables the interface and Juniper interfaces are enabled by default, the proper translation would be to simply omit any disable command. \\n\\nIf you specifically need the negation of shutdown (equivalent to \"no shutdown\"), there would typically be no command needed in Juniper (as interfaces are up by default), or you would use `delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable` if the interface was previously disable